            <ArticleTitle>Spinal epidural hematoma mimicking subarachnoid hemorrhage: A case study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>182</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/sni.sni_197_17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Spontaneous spinal epidural hematoma (SSEH) is a rare spinal cord disorder requiring urgent diagnosis and prompt treatment to prevent irreversible neurological damage. Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), usually presenting with headache and neck stiffness, is also a worldwide neurosurgical emergency. In this case study, a patient on clopidogrel presented with an SSEH mimicking a spontaneous SAH.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE DESCRIPTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 74-year-old female presented with severe headache and neck pain which ultimately attributed to a cervicothoracic SSEH resulting in cord compression. Notably, there was a long delay in establishing the diagnosis of SSEH as her symptoms mimicked a SAH. Although the patient ultimately underwent spinal surgery followed by intense rehabilitation, the diagnostic delay resulted in a poor neurological outcome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Patients with spontaneous cervicothoracic epidural hematomas may occasionally present with symptoms of severe headache and neck pain/stiffness mimicking an SAH. Long diagnostic delays in establishing SSEH may result in irreversible cord damage.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
